# The words of a package's answer that the list judges by their WHOLE spelling,
# and what becomes of each (design.md §4.19; panel 198). `libraries.filter_words`
# walks the answer and asks here before it asks about prefixes. Its own module
# because `cli/libraries.hero` stands at its ceiling, as `cli/package_answer.hero`
# is.
#
# The property the list asks of a word is `libraries.hero`'s: does it name a file,
# load anything, or write anything at build time? A word is admitted here by name,
# never by a shape, because no test of a shape is complete (`-Wl,@file`,
# `-Wl,--plugin`, `-Wl,-Map=<file>` each pass a test that asks whether a word
# names a path), and every spelling is the whole word, so a comma that tunnels a
# second option into it (`-Wl,--enable-new-dtags,--plugin,x.so`) is a different
# word and is refused.
#
# **A word PASSES** when clang gives it the one meaning on every platform
# measured and it reaches a compile and a link as it is: `-pthread`, a driver
# spelling of `-D_REENTRANT` and `-lpthread`, which the list already admits.
#
# **A word is LEFT OUT** when it restates what the compiler's own link line
# already says, so passing it adds nothing and one linker refuses it: Apple's
# `ld` stops at `--enable-new-dtags` and at `--export-dynamic` (*ld: unknown
# options*), and `lld-link` warns. Debian's GNU ld 2.44 and LLD 22.1.8 and
# Ubuntu's GNU ld 2.42 write the same bytes with and without them:
# `--enable-new-dtags` is the default tag those toolchains write beside the
# admitted `-Wl,-rpath` (a GNU ld built with upstream's default writes RPATH
# there, the tag a package that says only `-Wl,-rpath` already gets on it), and
# `--export-dynamic` is what clang hands the linker for the `-rdynamic` every
# POSIX link carries (`flags.link_flags`). The word that MOVES a tag,
# `--disable-new-dtags`, is not here. Both premises are asked where they can be:
# `tests/harness/absence.hero`'s *an admitted -Wl,-rpath leaves a RUNPATH* and
# the test below on `-rdynamic`.
#
# **`-isystem <dir>` is handed on as `-I<dir>`** (`spelled`), its directory joined
# or as the next word, as clang reads it (Apple clang 21: `-isystem/x` is
# `-isystem` and `/x`). clang quiets every diagnostic LOCATED IN a header it finds
# by `-isystem`, and only those: measured on Apple clang 21 and Debian clang
# 22.1.8, the program's own lines, a header macro expanded in them among them,
# keep every `-Werror` the compiler sets, and the header's own inline code loses
# them. A library's header is judged under those flags as `ffi_header_refused`
# says (*no warning is turned off for a header*) and as panel 188's
# compiler-engineer argued from (`-isystem` is refused, so no bound header is in
# a system directory), so the word is read as the directory it names and the
# guarantee stands. Seven headers under Debian 13's `/usr/include/mit-krb5`, the
# one directory its nine `-isystem` packages name, compile under `-I` with the
# compiler's flags (krb5.h, gssapi/gssapi.h, gssapi/gssapi_krb5.h, gssrpc/rpc.h,
# kadm5/admin.h, kdb.h, profile.h; panel 198's compiler-engineer);
# gssapi/gssapi_alloc.h does not (implicit malloc and free), and none of the six
# entry headers a binding names includes it (panel 198's critic).
#
# **`-Wl,-rpath,<dir>` stays where `eac3e5b5` put it**, and the argument it was
# admitted on stands beside the list again, as that commit (2026-08-14, no
# sitting) wrote it beside `ALLOWED` in the Rust and the port dropped it: an rpath
# names no file, loads nothing and writes nothing at build time and changes what
# the loader picks at run time; a hostile `.pc` saying `-L/tmp/x -lfoo` already
# chooses the library, so it is no new capability, but it lets the choice be
# swapped AFTER the build by anyone who can write that directory. The answer (c)
# of 2026-08-15 left that class to the corpus timeout and refused the list a
# second half: *a guard is not widened to cover a class it cannot see*. So no word
# is admitted here for what it does at run time. **On Windows the rpath is left
# out** (`rpath_words`): a PE loader has none, and `lld-link` warns on `-rpath`
# and then reads its value as an INPUT FILE, so `-Wl,-rpath,h.o` linked an object
# the list never admitted (panel 198's ffi-pragmatist, clang 23.1.1).
#
# **`-pthread` reaches a COMPILE everywhere and a LINK everywhere but Windows**,
# where clang 23.1.1 for `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` calls it at a link *argument
# unused during compilation* (measured 2026-10-08): a warning on a correct
# program. The question is the one `flags.link_flags` asks, `exe_suffix()`.
#
# What is not here stays refused, and the refusal's note is `libraries.refused`.

use cli/process
use cli/flags
use cli/strings

# Whether `word` is passed on as it is.
function passes(word: str) -> bool
    return word == "-pthread"

# The flag a package's valued word is handed to clang as: `-isystem` is `-I`.
function spelled(word: str) -> str
    if word == "-isystem"
        return "-I"
    return word

# The flags clang reads with their value joined or as the next word, every one
# in both spellings (defect 160); `-isystem`'s value is handed on as `-I`'s
# (`spelled`). Moved here from `libraries.valued` and `allowed_prefixed`, with
# `-isystem`, when panel 198 took `cli/libraries.hero` past its ceiling.
constant VALUED: [str]
    [
        "-D"
        "-U"
        "-I"
        "-L"
        "-l"
        "-F"
        "-isystem"
    ]

# Whether `word` is one of `VALUED` alone, its value in the next word.
function valued(word: str) -> bool
    for flag in VALUED
        if word == flag
            return true

    return false

# The one word a package's `word` is handed on as where the list admits it in one
# word: `-pthread` as it is, and a `VALUED` flag with its value joined,
# `-isystem`'s as `-I`'s; an error where the list does not.
function one_word(word: str) -> str?
    if passes(word)
        return ok(word)

    for flag in VALUED
        if strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: flag) && word.len() > flag.len()
            return ok(spelled(flag) + after(text: word, from: flag.len()))

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a word the list admits whole")

function after(text: str, from: i64) -> str
    cs = text.chars()
    return cs.slice(from: from, to: cs.len()).join("")

# Whether a LINK takes `word` as it is: `-pthread`, where clang does not call it unused.
function link_passes(word: str) -> bool
    return passes(word) && process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"

# Whether `word` says nothing the compiler's link line does not already say.
function restates(word: str) -> bool
    return word == "-Wl,--enable-new-dtags" || word == "-Wl,--export-dynamic"

# What an admitted rpath becomes: the one word, or none where the linker would
# read its value as a file.
function rpath_words(value: str) -> [str]
    if process.exe_suffix() == ".exe"
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    return ["-Wl,-rpath," + value]

test "a word is passed or left out by its whole spelling, and nothing near it is"
    assert passes("-pthread")
    assert !passes("-pthreads")
    assert !passes("-pthread=1")
    assert !passes("-Wl,-pthread")
    assert !passes("")
    assert link_passes("-pthread") == (process.exe_suffix() != ".exe")
    assert !link_passes("-pthreads")
    assert restates("-Wl,--enable-new-dtags")
    assert restates("-Wl,--export-dynamic")
    assert spelled("-isystem") == "-I"
    assert spelled("-I") == "-I"
    assert spelled("-isystem-after") == "-isystem-after"
    assert one_word("-isystem/sys/a").must() == "-I/sys/a"
    assert one_word("-I/inc").must() == "-I/inc"
    assert one_word("-lz").must() == "-lz"
    assert one_word("-pthread").must() == "-pthread"
    assert one_word("-isystem").is_err()
    assert one_word("-iquote/sys/a").is_err()
    assert one_word("-pthreads").is_err()
    assert valued("-isystem")
    assert !valued("-isystem/sys/a")
    assert !valued("-iquote")

    # The word that moves a tag, a comma that carries a second option, and the
    # position-dependent words are not restatements of anything.
    assert !restates("-Wl,--disable-new-dtags")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--enable-new-dtags,--plugin,x.so")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--export-dynamic,-e,evil")
    assert !restates("--enable-new-dtags")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--no-as-needed")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--whole-archive")
    assert !restates("-Xlinker")

# Where the rpath means something it is one word, and on Windows none.
test "an admitted rpath is one word where a loader reads it, and none on Windows"
    if process.exe_suffix() == ".exe"
        assert rpath_words("/opt/lib").len() == 0

    if process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        assert rpath_words("/opt/lib") == ["-Wl,-rpath,/opt/lib"]

# The premise of the second word `restates` holds, asked of the line itself: the
# compiler's own POSIX link already carries `-rdynamic`, which clang hands the
# linker as `-export-dynamic`, so a package's `-Wl,--export-dynamic` adds nothing
# (Debian GNU ld 2.44 and LLD 22.1.8: the same bytes with and without it). The day
# `flags.link_flags` stops saying it, the word restates nothing and this fires.
test "the link line carries -rdynamic on every POSIX link, which --export-dynamic restates"
    if process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        said: bool @ false

        for flag in flags.link_flags()
            if flag == "-rdynamic"
                said @ true

        assert said
